{"event":"trap-result","runId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-0/branch-10","trapId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-0/branch-10/trap-0","ordinal":0,"result":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.16345","title":"DRAMA: Dissecting Attention Redundancy for Accelerating Multimodal Diffusion Large Language Models","authors":"Yi-Hsin Hung , Fangfu Liu , Shao-Yuan Lo , Chu-Song Chen","submitted":"12 Nov 2026","abstract":"Multimodal diffusion large language models (dLLMs) generate text through iterative masked denoising with bidirectional attention, offering a compelling alternative to autoregressive vision-language modeling. However, each denoising step requires a full forward pass over long multimodal sequences in which visual tokens dominate the sequence length, and a fixed step budget further compounds this quadratic per-step cost. Existing inference acceleration approaches for multimodal dLLMs characterize attention redundancy implicitly, motivating designs from isolated attention visualizations rather than from measurement. We close this gap with DRAMA, a training-free acceleration framework grounded in a systematic dissection of attention redundancy in multimodal dLLMs. By probing attention tensors along spatial and temporal axes, we reveal two persistent redundancy structures: (i) attention mass consistently concentrates on a sparse subset of visual tokens, and (ii) attention distributions rapidly stabilize across denoising steps after an initial formation phase. These findings motivate two complementary mechanisms: Spatial-Redundancy-Aware Visual Token Pruning (SVTP) and Sufficiency-Guided Adaptive Stopping (SGAS). Together, DRAMA achieves an average 18.67 times inference speedup across six vision-language benchmarks without training, architectural modification, or attention caching, while maintaining competitive accuracy. Our results establish a strong, measurement-grounded acceleration baseline for multimodal dLLM inference.","numbers":["Together, DRAMA achieves an average 18.67 times inference speedup across six vision-language benchmarks without training, architectural modification, or attention caching, while maintaining competitive accuracy."],"method":[],"caveats":["However, each denoising step requires a full forward pass over long multimodal sequences in which visual tokens dominate the sequence length, and a fixed step budget further compounds this quadratic per-step cost."],"topics":["architecture-attention","vision-multimodal","diffusion"],"score":12,"fullText":true,"readChars":6777,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16345","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.16345"}}
{"event":"branch-done","runId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-0/branch-10","parentForkId":"briefing-proof3-2026-11-16/fork-0","branch":10,"outcome":{"ok":true,"value":{"ok":true,"error":"","id":"2611.16345","title":"DRAMA: Dissecting Attention Redundancy for Accelerating Multimodal Diffusion Large Language Models","authors":"Yi-Hsin Hung , Fangfu Liu , Shao-Yuan Lo , Chu-Song Chen","submitted":"12 Nov 2026","abstract":"Multimodal diffusion large language models (dLLMs) generate text through iterative masked denoising with bidirectional attention, offering a compelling alternative to autoregressive vision-language modeling. However, each denoising step requires a full forward pass over long multimodal sequences in which visual tokens dominate the sequence length, and a fixed step budget further compounds this quadratic per-step cost. Existing inference acceleration approaches for multimodal dLLMs characterize attention redundancy implicitly, motivating designs from isolated attention visualizations rather than from measurement. We close this gap with DRAMA, a training-free acceleration framework grounded in a systematic dissection of attention redundancy in multimodal dLLMs. By probing attention tensors along spatial and temporal axes, we reveal two persistent redundancy structures: (i) attention mass consistently concentrates on a sparse subset of visual tokens, and (ii) attention distributions rapidly stabilize across denoising steps after an initial formation phase. These findings motivate two complementary mechanisms: Spatial-Redundancy-Aware Visual Token Pruning (SVTP) and Sufficiency-Guided Adaptive Stopping (SGAS). Together, DRAMA achieves an average 18.67 times inference speedup across six vision-language benchmarks without training, architectural modification, or attention caching, while maintaining competitive accuracy. Our results establish a strong, measurement-grounded acceleration baseline for multimodal dLLM inference.","numbers":["Together, DRAMA achieves an average 18.67 times inference speedup across six vision-language benchmarks without training, architectural modification, or attention caching, while maintaining competitive accuracy."],"method":[],"caveats":["However, each denoising step requires a full forward pass over long multimodal sequences in which visual tokens dominate the sequence length, and a fixed step budget further compounds this quadratic per-step cost."],"topics":["architecture-attention","vision-multimodal","diffusion"],"score":12,"fullText":true,"readChars":6777,"absUrl":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.16345","htmlUrl":"https://ar5iv.org/html/2611.16345"}}}
